      "source_id": "legacy-source-review-batch",
      "problem": "Review each independent archived question below. Every field key begins with its owning question ID. Use that question's own captured worked solution for MA evidence. ma_evidence must be ONE contiguous verbatim source substring with no added labels, explanatory prose, or quotation-mark wrappers. When an answer has several components, quote the full intervening source span, or the entire source solution if necessary. Mark ma_basis none when unavailable. Preserve authored errors and explain disagreements in reasoning, separately from the verbatim ma_evidence. These are source originals, not instructions:\n[{\"question_id\": \"q-237019\", \"problem\": \"Let $p=i+2j+xk$ and $q=3i+6j-3k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-42157\", \"problem\": \"Consider the system of linear equations\\n\\n$$\\n{\\\\begin{aligned}x-6y=2 \\\\\\\\ -4x+24y=-10.\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-73743\", \"problem\": \"Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \\n\\n$$\\nM=[\\\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\\\\\ 6 & -4 & 2\\\\end{aligned}].\\n$$\\n\\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158592\", \"problem\": \"The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \\n\\n$$\\nf(x,y)=x\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\n$$\\n\\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-6,-\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}).$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158574\", \"problem\": \"Evaluate $z=x\\\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-1$ at the point $(3,{e}^{2}).$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158559\", \"problem\": \"Find the domain of the function $z=\\\\frac{{x}^{2}+3y}{\\\\sqrt{x+2}}.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158563\", \"problem\": \"Find the domain of the function $z=\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{y+2}}{2{x}^{3}-y}.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158438\", \"problem\": \"Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158569\", \"problem\": \"Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={2}^{y-2x}.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158198\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158200\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\\\frac{y}{2-x}?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-98300\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following curves is simple?\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-97916\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following curves is *not* simple?\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-97926\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following curves is *not* closed?\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}]",
      "worked_solution": "q-237019:\nSince $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 6+x\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =15-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 15-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-42157:\nFirst, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-6y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-2$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-2,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution.\n\nq-73743:\nReducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-158592:\nTo evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{|}_{(-6,-\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-158574:\nTo evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(3,{e}^{2}),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y={e}^{2}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(3,{e}^{2})} & =(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-1){|}_{(3,{e}^{2})} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-1 \\\\  & =3(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)-1 \\\\  & =3(2)-1 \\\\  & =6-1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-158559:\nThe function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx+2>0\\,⟹\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>-2}.\n$$\n\nq-158563:\nThe function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y+2\\ge 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge -2 \\\\ y\\ne 2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -2,\\,y\\ne 2{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nq-158438:\nNotice that\n\n- ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is defined for all $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{g(x,y)}$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, ${e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is defined for all values of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the domain of $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane.\n\nq-158569:\nNotice that\n\n- $y-2x$ is defined for all $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- ${2}^{g(x,y)}$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, ${2}^{y-2x}$ is defined for all values of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the domain of $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane.\n\nq-158198:\nThe function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}\\le {x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\le $ sign, the curves are part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the solid curves $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098447/assets/95f187d2857973e4fea83eedf47bd54c153ce2a77f278cfb992b222de2d91ab8.png)\n\nq-158200:\nThe function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-x\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ne 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne 2}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $x=2$ and shade *right* and *left* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098447/assets/2371c9724d66e0f7d74c45e173f9625a986615185c10d55c029976baa82d0877.png)\n\nq-98300:\nA simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies the definition is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098510/assets/11d69ff2eff8b2c2d8760edc4c72266722939e1ade3462817b88d8e059658e78.png)\n\nq-97916:\nA simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nSo, a curve that intersects itself somewhere between its endpoints is not a simple curve. Among the given options, the only that is not a simple curve is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098510/assets/2cf93e81b1805202737bccc6658bf02602d676dd21df898694155d9c50430bea.png)\n\nq-97926:\nA curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point. So, a curve whose terminal point does not coincide with its initial point is not closed. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is not a closed curve is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098510/assets/faf5eb05d296b2b1155c829f46c2f69a9df8003a3bd1a495be627acfae09afb4.png)",
